============================================================================
3. The symmetry group, and whether it forces t/T = k/12
============================================================================
  symmetries of the form (spatial g, time shift m*T/12):
    identity       with shift  0/12 of a period   mismatch 0.00e+00
    identity       with shift  4/12 of a period   mismatch 7.04e-08
    identity       with shift  8/12 of a period   mismatch 7.04e-08
    reflect in y   with shift  2/12 of a period   mismatch 8.78e-08
    reflect in y   with shift  6/12 of a period   mismatch 7.04e-08
    reflect in y   with shift 10/12 of a period   mismatch 8.76e-08
  group order found: 6

  The time shifts that occur are [0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10], which generate the cyclic group
  of order 12 acting on the period. A symmetry maps isosceles
  configurations to isosceles configurations, so that group permutes
  the twelve of them; acting transitively, it forces them to be evenly
  spaced, which is what t/T = k/12 says.
